<% content_for :page_title, "Regexp help" %>

<%
  code_class = "rounded bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs"
  sections = [
    { title: "Building blocks", rows: [
        [ ".", "Any single character" ],
        [ "\\d  \\s", "A digit; whitespace (space, tab, line break)" ],
        [ "[abc]  [a-z]", "One of these characters; one in this range" ],
        [ "[^abc]", "Any character except these" ],
        [ "\\p{L}", "Any letter, in any script — ü, é, ж and 字 included" ],
        [ "\\.  \\?  \\(", "A literal dot, question mark, bracket: put \\ before . ? * + ( ) [ ] { } | ^ $ \\" ]
      ] },
    { title: "How often", rows: [
        [ "?", "Optional: zero or one time — colou?r matches color and colour" ],
        [ "*  +", "Zero or more; one or more times" ],
        [ "{2,5}", "Between 2 and 5 times" ]
      ] },
    { title: "Where", rows: [
        [ "\\b", "A word boundary — \\bseo\\b matches “seo” but not “seoul”" ],
        [ "\\A  \\z", "The very start; the very end of the text" ],
        [ "^  $", "The start; the end of a line (not only of the whole text)" ]
      ] },
    { title: "Alternatives and groups", rows: [
        [ "a|b", "Either one — crypto|nft|airdrop" ],
        [ "(…)", "Groups a part, so ? * + or | apply to all of it — (free|cheap)\\s+followers" ]
      ] },
    { title: "Upper and lower case", rows: [
        [ "(?i)", "Case-insensitive. Watchwords already ignore case, so (?i) — familiar from other tools — is accepted but changes nothing" ],
        [ "(?-i)", "Case-sensitive from here on — (?-i)SEO matches “SEO” but not “seo”" ],
        [ "(?-i:…)", "Case-sensitive for just this group" ]
      ] }
  ]
%>

<h1 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Regexp help</h1>

<p class="mb-6 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
  With the match set to <strong>Regex</strong>, the pattern is a Ruby regular
  expression, checked against each applicant's reason, profile note, username
  and display name. The “Word” and “Substring” matches need none of this: they
  take the pattern literally.
</p>

<div class="space-y-6">
  <% sections.each do |section| %>
    <section class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white dark:bg-stone-900 p-5">
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><%= section[:title] %></h2>
      <dl class="divide-y divide-stone-200 dark:divide-stone-800 text-sm">
        <% section[:rows].each do |syntax, description| %>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-4 py-2">
            <dt class="w-28 shrink-0"><code class="<%= code_class %> whitespace-pre"><%= syntax %></code></dt>
            <dd class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300"><%= description %></dd>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </dl>
    </section>
  <% end %>

  <section class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white dark:bg-stone-900 p-5">
    <h2 class="mb-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Tips</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>
        <code class="<%= code_class %>">\w</code> only covers the letters a–z, digits and “_”, so
        <code class="<%= code_class %>">\w+</code> stops at the “ü” in “müller”. Use
        <code class="<%= code_class %>">\p{L}</code> for letters in any language.
      </li>
      <li>
        Allow for spacing and spelling tricks: <code class="<%= code_class %>">free\s*money</code> also
        catches “freemoney”, <code class="<%= code_class %>">cr[yi]pto</code> catches “cripto”.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep it simple. A pattern that takes longer than <%= (KeywordRule::REGEX_TIMEOUT * 1000).to_i %> ms on
        a text simply doesn't match it; nested repeats such as <code class="<%= code_class %>">(a+)+</code>
        are the usual cause.
      </li>
      <li>
        A pattern that isn't valid is refused when you try to save it.
        Matches are highlighted on the request page, which is the quickest way to
        check a new watchword does what you meant.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
